This page prepares the application that Bitrise will build. Start with your application folder on your Mac. By the end, you should have a working local app, passing project checks, and a published source revision you can identify.

**Work location — your application repository on your Mac.** The enrollment request will be created later in `mobile-platform-configuration`.

Use [Machine Setup](../onboarding/machine-setup-guide/index.mdx) to install the required tools on macOS Apple Silicon. Follow the application's documented versions and dependency installation instructions.

Prepare your application code, tests, and CAMP file here. Platform Engineering supplies the Bitrise workflow and shared CI/CD automation through the selected template.

## 1. Choose your starting point

| Your situation | What to do |
|---|---|
| You already have an application repository | Open that checkout and continue with step 2. |
| You need a new application | Select your platform in **Create a new application** below. |
| Your application is already enrolled in Bitrise | Prepare and verify your source changes, then follow the later-build procedure for its existing enrollment. |

### Create a new application

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React Native">

**Create the app from the approved React Native starter.**

1. Create an empty private application repository through the approved process. Save its clone URL for step 5.
2. Follow the approved React Native starter README to set up package registry access. Confirm the CLI and starter versions it requires.
3. Open Terminal in the **parent folder where you want the new project created**. Run the workshop starter command with `MyChickenapp` as the project name. The command resolves the latest CLI. If the current starter README provides a pinned command, use that version with the same project name.

```bash
npx @react-native-community/cli@latest init MyChickenapp \
  --template @mobilearch/react-native-template
```

The generator creates a folder named `MyChickenapp`. For your own project, substitute your approved project name in both commands.

**Generated React Native project**

```text
MyChickenapp/
├── package.json
├── <package-manager lockfile>
├── <React Native source and test files>
├── android/
│   ├── gradlew
│   └── app/
└── ios/
    └── Podfile
```

**React Native starter structure**

The starter repository's `docs/template-structure.md` shows the following layout under `template/`. This is the starter's source layout. After generation, open `MyChickenapp/` to work on your app.

```text
template/
├── src/
│   ├── app/
│   │   ├── bootstrap/
│   │   ├── entry/
│   │   ├── navigation/
│   │   ├── providers/
│   │   └── store/
│   ├── features/
│   │   ├── auth/
│   │   ├── catalog/
│   │   ├── tasks/
│   │   └── settings/
│   ├── shared/
│   │   ├── infrastructure/
│   │   ├── ui/
│   │   └── types/
│   ├── application/
│   ├── domain/
│   └── data/
├── __tests__/
└── docs/
```

| Folder | What belongs there |
|---|---|
| `src/app/` | App startup, dependency setup, Redux store, session setup, providers, and navigation. Start tracing the app at `index.js` and `src/app/entry/AppRoot.tsx`. |
| `src/features/` | Screens, ViewModels, and state owned by each feature. The starter includes Auth, Catalog, Tasks, and Settings as reference features. |
| `src/shared/` | Reusable UI, shared types, and infrastructure such as the API client, app configuration, and storage adapter. |
| `src/application/` | Helpers that coordinate work across the app. |
| `src/domain/` | Business entities, failures, repository contracts, and use cases. |
| `src/data/` | Data models, mappers, data sources, and repository implementations. |
| `__tests__/` | Starter tests, including an app render smoke test and an auth reducer test. |

4. Enter the generated folder and confirm the expected files exist. If you changed the project name above, use the same name here.

```bash
cd MyChickenapp
pwd
ls -l package.json android/gradlew ios/Podfile
```

5. Continue to **Open the application folder** below. Run the app and its local checks, then publish the generated source to your private repo and record the branch and commit.

The React Native starter generates the app. During enrollment, the approved Bitrise template supplies the shared `bitrise.yml`.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Android">

**Create a native Android app.**

If your team provides an approved Android application starter, follow its README. For the native `MyChickenapp` example, use the Android Studio **Empty Activity** template for Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.

1. In Android Studio, select **New Project → Empty Activity**.
2. Set **Name** to `MyChickenapp`. Use the package name and minimum SDK approved for your app. Save it in a new `MyChickenapp` folder and finish project creation.
3. Wait for Gradle sync. Open a terminal in the generated folder and build the debug APK:

```bash
pwd
ls -l gradlew
./gradlew :app:assembleDebug
```

The command assumes the generated module is named `app`. In Android Studio, select an emulator and click **Run** to confirm the starter screen opens. [Android Studio project setup](https://developer.android.com/studio/projects/create-project)

**Example Android folder structure**

```text
MyChickenapp/
├── gradlew                         # Runs the project's Gradle wrapper
├── gradle/
│   └── wrapper/
├── settings.gradle.kts             # Declares the Gradle modules
├── build.gradle.kts                # Project-level build configuration
└── app/
    ├── build.gradle.kts            # App module configuration
    └── src/
        ├── main/                   # Kotlin source, manifest, and resources
        ├── test/                   # Local unit tests, when present
        └── androidTest/            # Device and emulator tests, when present
```

Your starter may use `.gradle` instead of `.gradle.kts` or a different module name. Open the folder that contains `gradlew`.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

**Create a native iOS app.**

If your team provides an approved iOS application starter, follow its README. For the native `MyChickenapp` example, use Xcode’s **iOS App** template.

1. In Xcode, choose **File → New → Project** and select **iOS → App**.
2. Set **Product Name** to `MyChickenapp`, **Interface** to **SwiftUI**, and **Language** to **Swift**. Use your approved organization identifier and team settings. Save the project.
3. In a terminal beside the generated project file, confirm that Xcode can list the targets and schemes:

```bash
pwd
xcodebuild -list -project MyChickenapp.xcodeproj
```

In Xcode, select the app scheme and an iOS Simulator, then click **Run** to confirm the starter screen opens. [Create an Xcode project](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/creating-an-xcode-project-for-an-app)

**Example iOS folder structure**

```text
MyChickenapp/
├── MyChickenapp.xcodeproj/          # Xcode project and target configuration
├── MyChickenapp/
│   ├── MyChickenappApp.swift       # App entry point
│   ├── ContentView.swift          # Starter screen
│   └── Assets.xcassets/            # App icons, images, and colors
├── MyChickenappTests/             # Unit tests, if selected
└── MyChickenappUITests/           # UI tests, if selected
```

Open `MyChickenapp.xcodeproj` for this example. If your starter specifies an `.xcworkspace`, open that workspace.

